<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Biridiya</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">14th century BCE</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Biridiya was the ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-megiddo">Megiddo</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a> in the fourteenth century BCE, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a>. Several of his letters survive in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-archive">Amarna archive</a>, in which he stresses his loyalty, reports on labour provided for Egyptian estates and describes his conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/labayu">Labayu</a>, ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shechem">Shechem</a>, in whose capture he took part.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/biridiya.jpg" alt="Biridiya" width="320" height="388" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amarna_letters._Letter_from_Biridiya,_King_of_Megiddo,_to_the_Egyptian_Pharaoh_Amenhotep_III_or_his_son_Akhenaten._14th_century_BCE._From_Tell_el-Amarna,_Egypt._British_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
